The idea of having a 'repeatable' version of nethack has been tossed
around many a year. The suggested solution of reseeding the RNG the
same way in every game has been shot down numerous times, because, as
we all know, the same RNG is used to control level generation, monster
behaviour, item effects, etc, etc, etc. A small change in what you do
in one part of the game will mean an extra random number generated at
one point, which will throw all the subsequent numbers out of kilter,
right?

OK, so what if you have separate RNGs for every task. A lot of work you
say... well, there is a way to hack a solution. I've written a patch
which keeps track of a separate seed value for every '.c' file in the
source. It is managed by declaring a static variable in hack.h, which
is included in every file. Then I #define some wrappers for the
functions in rnd.c which also pass in the seed, and Hey Presto:
repeatable nethack.

The patch is available here:
http://www.cse.unsw.edu.au/~malcolmr/nethack/3.4.3/rand.diff

It was just done as a crazy experiement. It has no option for selecting
the particular seed to use, so it will always play the 'same' game. It
is by no means portable, but it should run with anything that uses the
GNU C lib.

There is a real feeling of deja-vu playing this game over and over.
Things are the same, yet not quite the same. The map is the same, and a
monster will appear in the same spot as last time, but it will be a
different monster. It's weird.

